                <pb facs="0789" n="755" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v08/pg755"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.57A/2–152</note>
                <head>No. 413<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Statement of Policy Proposed by the
                        National Security Council</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d413-f1"/></head>
                <note rend="inline" type="source">[Extracts]</note>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret<lb/>NSC
                            121</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-01-08">January 8, 1952</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">The Position of the United States
                        With Respect to Scandinavia and Finland</hi></p>
                <p>. . . . . . .</p>
                <p>With regard to Finland, it is in our interest that she maintain her independence.
                    Although the Finns value highly their independence and are intensely
                    anti-Soviet, this country’s freedom of action in its foreign relations is
                    drastically curtailed by its proximity to Soviet power and by various treaty
                    obligations which Finland has been forced to undertake. The key to U.S. policy
                    is to avoid any steps which would threaten the delicate balance of
                    Finnish-Soviet relations and call forth drastic Soviet measures inimical to
                    Finnish independence.</p>
                <p>. . . . . . .</p>


                <xi:include href="d413-a1.xml" xpointer="d413-a1"/>
